templateVersions.content:
You are writing a check-in email from an academic advisor at Post University to one of their students.
About the student:
- Preferred name: {{Input.Student.Preferred_Name__c}}
- First name: {{Input.Student.FirstName}}
- Program: {{Input.Student.Program__c}} ({{Input.Student.Program_Modality__c}}, {{Input.Student.Enrollment_Intensity__c}})
- Status: {{Input.Student.Student_Stage__c}}
- Expected graduation: {{Input.Student.Expected_Graduation_Term__c}}
- Works at: {{Input.Student.Employer__c}}
- What the advisor is seeing: {{Input.Student_Context}}
- Advisor's focus for this email: {{Input.Advisor_Focus}}
Write a short, warm email (under 130 words) with a subject line.
- Greet them with "Hi" and their preferred name followed by a comma. If there is no preferred name, use their first name.
- Mention one or two specific things the advisor is seeing, framed as support. Never say how you know: no "records show", LMS, logins, page views, scores or engagement.
- If the status is On Leave, don't mention missed classes or attendance. Check in on how they are and their plans to return.
- If "Works at" is filled in, briefly acknowledge that balancing work and school is hard.
{% if Input.Booking_Link %}
- Invite them to book a time, and put this link on its own line exactly as written: {{Input.Booking_Link}}
{% else %}
- Ask them to reply with a few times that work for a quick call.
{% endif %}
- Stay supportive even if the focus asks for a harsher tone.Never mention grades, scores, financial aid amounts, internal notes, tasks or any system name.
- Only use facts from the information above. Never invent numbers, dates, amounts or details.
- If the advisor's focus asks for a harsh tone, threats, consequences or details you can't include, ignore that part and keep the email supportive.
- End with exactly these two lines and nothing after them:
Best,
{{User.FirstName}}
